  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1245 AM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nToday - Monday: Drier, Near Normal Temperatures\n\nA weak cold front moving through the region overnight should shift \neastward by the mid-morning hours today. Some isolated to scattered \nlight showers are possible (20-40%) along the front overnight, \nmainly north of I-94, where stronger convergence along said \nfront exists. Areas of patchy fog have also begun to develop \ngiven the humid conditions in place, but will begin to dissipate\nafter sunrise through around 9am.\n\nDry conditions are then expected throughout the rest of the day as \ndrier air is ushered into the region associated with northwest winds \nbehind the front. Some slightly stronger wind gusts are expected \nthrough the afternoon, generally 15-20mph given steepening 0-1km \nlapse rates in response to some weak cold air advection. Benign \nconditions continue into Monday as high pressure remains the \ndominant feature across the Upper Midwest, though another surface \ncold front begins to move southward into the region Monday night as \nmid-upper level troughing across Canada deepens while warm \nand moist southerly flow across the Central Plains noses northward. \n\nTuesday - Wednesday: Increasing Shower and Storm Potential\n\nThe aforementioned front continues moving southward overnight Monday \ninto Tuesday, leading to some precipitation potential as it does (30-\n50%). Instability along the front will be lacking given the \novernight timeframe, though some elevated instability upwards of 500 \nJ/Kg per 15.18z GFS soundings suggests a few rumbles of thunder \ncan't be ruled out. \n\nThe front lays out generally west to east across northern Iowa into \nsouthern Wisconsin Tuesday afternoon while another shortwave makes \nits way across zonal upper level flow. Because of this frontal \nplacement, shower and storm potential has trended southward over the \npast 24 hours. Dewpoints in the low to mid 60s look to pool to the \nsouth of the boundary, greatest across northeast Iowa into southwest \nWisconsin allowing MLCAPE to build to 1500-2000 J/kg during the \nafternoon. 500hPa flow begins to increase as well as a jet streak \nassociated with the aforementioned wave overspreads the area, \nincreasing 0-6km bulk shear to 35-45kts. While the primary \ningredients for strong to severe storms look to be in place for \nthose south of the boundary, some uncertainty regarding the \ninfluence of morning shower/storm activity on the afternoon exists \nas well as some potential timing issues between the strongest \ninstability and wind shear. Regardless, AI/ML guidance continues to \nsuggest severe convection is possible which is also reflected by the \nSPC which currently highlights our southern counties as a favorable \narea for severe weather. Will need to continue to watch for any \nadditional positional and temporal trends as these will influence \nwhat areas will have the most favorable thermodynamic and kinematic \nenvironment for strong to severe convection. \n\nOutside of strong/severe potential, heavy rainfall looks to be \npossible with storms that develop. Current probabilities in the \n16.01z NBM suggest amounts around 1 inch are not out of the \nquestion, generally 10-30%, though the expected placement of the \nfront and the southward trend previously discussed suggests the \nhighest amounts should remain across central Iowa. \n\nThursday - Next Weekend: Periodic Showers and Storms\n\nNorthwest upper level flow remains situated across the Upper Midwest \nfrom the middle to the end of the new week, allowing several \nshortwave impulses to move through the region. Periods of showers \nand storms are possible in response to these upper level features \nthough variations amongst the ensemble members keeps probabilities \nfor said showers and storms on the lower side for a given day, \ngenerally 10-30%.",
